Ross Presser <rpresser@NOSPAMgmail.com.invalid> writes:
> > Every lost game carries a lesson, and mere persistence will avail you
> > nothing if you do not learn those lessons and take them to heart.
> 
> I guess the point I was trying to make, and that I have a hunch KPD would
> agree with, is that "learning those lessons" is the truly non-easy part of
> Nethack.

I'm not sure I agree. "Learning lessons" doesn't mean that you fully
understand every root cause of every YASD. It _does_ mean approaching
the game with the attitude that, if you keep doing the same things and
keep suffering early deaths, there's causality involved. It isn't
_just_ because the game is hard, or because you're unlucky. If you
want different results you need to modify your approach, observe the
results, and iterate.

For me, the non-easy part of nethack is the psychological game: the
discipline required to seize every advantage and avoid every avoidable
risk, even when doing so is tedious or the temptation is present to
"oh, just try it; there's only a 1-in-20 chance I'll get the bad
result" when that bad result could kill you. It's making myself make
_one_ move, observe the result, read the status line, *THINK* and only
then make the next move.

> All the spoilers and all the newsgroup discussion put together still
> do everything that's needed to train you to ascend.

They make it much easier, but IMHO are neither necessary nor
sufficient.  (Disclaimer: I've not ascended unspoiled, and it's too
late for me to try to do so now. On reflection, it seems as though it
would be quite hard but not impossible.)

> Perhaps we're just not as intelligent as you require us to be.

Now, now. I "require" (FVO approaching "suggest") only the
intelligence to try something different if the thing you're doing now
isn't working.

Of course, that's all predicated on the assumption that your goal is
to ascend. If you're not ascending but you're still having fun playing
the game, then there's need for neither discontent nor any change in
tactics.


